\subsection{Depth test}
\label{sec:f-depthtest}

\begin{definition}[hub null and excess B]
\label{def:hubnull}
Given a frame that assigns each centroid $x_i$ to a cluster $k(i)$ with hub $h_{k(i)}$, the cluster mean, write $x_i=h_{k(i)}+o_i$. The hub null keeps every offset $o_i$ and replaces the hubs by a Haar resample with their spectrum, $x'_i=h'_{k(i)}+o_i$, and the excess of Eq.~\ref{eq:excess} against this null, $e_B$, measures hierarchy above the clusters of the frame only.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[matched star]
\label{def:star}
The matched star of a cloud keeps the real clusters, each as a Haar sample with the cluster's own covariance, and places their hubs as Gaussian draws at the real hub radius, so it carries the clustering of the real cloud and no hierarchy above it. Its spectrum-matched-hub variant draws the hubs as a Haar resample of the real hubs instead.
\end{definition}

\paragraph{The depth test compares the real cloud with its matched star.} With $e_B$ read on the real cloud and on its matched star, the depth statistic and its standardized form are
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{depth}=e_B(X)-e_B(X_{\mathrm{star}}),\qquad z=\mathrm{depth}\,/\,\mathrm{s.d.},
\label{eq:depth}
\end{equation}
The spread is taken over star seeds and null replicates, and a cloud is certified as more hierarchical than a star when $z\le-2$. The test is needed because a star with no hierarchy passes the census of Definition~\ref{def:genuine}. The frame is the WordNet cut on ImageNet and the coarse labels on CIFAR-100, and the false-alarm rate and power are measured on real clouds in Section~\ref{sec:f-depth}.

\subsection{Comparing trees across models}
\label{sec:f-treemap}

\paragraph{Trees are compared under a configuration chosen before the comparison.} Each model's hierarchy is a dendrogram over the shared class centroids. Two hierarchies are compared by the adjusted Rand index between their cut partitions, by the correlation of their cophenetic distances, and by their agreement on which pair of a random class triplet merges first. The configuration of metric and linkage is selected per dataset by a criterion fixed without reference to the agreements: the highest cophenetic fidelity among configurations whose reference cuts are non-degenerate for every model. Local agreement is read with the permutation calibration of \citet{groger2026aristotelian}, on mutual-kNN agreement and on centroid CKA, with permutations of the class correspondence as the null.

\subsection{Projection and tasks}
\label{sec:f-projection}

\paragraph{Three readouts are compared on the intact representation.} A parameter-free map centers the features, scales the 95th-percentile norm to radius $1/\sqrt2$ and preserves directions. Euclidean, cosine and Poincar\'{e} readouts are then compared on the same representation. Nearest-centroid classification and 5-way 5-shot episodes score the three readouts on every cell of the task grid. % exp1, exp2

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:f-setup}

\paragraph{The census has twelve backbones, six class sets and sixteen text models.} The vision backbones are four supervised ViTs trained on ImageNet-21k, DINO-B, four DINOv2 sizes, CLIP-B, CLIP-L and SigLIP-B. The class sets are ImageNet and CIFAR-100, which carry a real hierarchy, CIFAR-10 and DTD, and the flat FMNIST and MNIST. The text models read the thousand ImageNet class names: four GPT-2 sizes, three Pythia sizes, two OLMo sizes and seven sentence embedders, which also read DBpedia Classes and HierarCaps. Table~\ref{tab:q14-panel} lists the panel.

\paragraph{Four controls test the alternatives.} Two self-supervised ResNet-50 backbones read the vision census. Two ViT-B trained on ImageNet-1k leaf labels, DeiT-B and the augreg ViT-B, test whether a hierarchical label set explains the depth. MERU and its Euclidean CLIP twin, in three sizes, test whether training in hyperbolic space creates depth. The census ViT-B fine-tuned with and without a hierarchical objective is a trained control.

\paragraph{The premise is read on images and hierarchy on centroids.} A cell is one pair of model and class set. The sample-level reading uses about a thousand stratified training images per cell on CIFAR-100 and DTD. Class centroids average a hundred cached training images per class on ImageNet and the full cached split elsewhere. Causal language models give the last-token state of ``a photo of a \{class\}'', extracted one prompt at a time because batching alters GPT-2's hidden states, and embedders use their recommended pooling (Table~\ref{tab:q2-text}).

\paragraph{Every reading is calibrated against two hundred replicates.} Each cell is read against two hundred Haar replicates, with half a million sampled quadruples per seed and ten seeds. Benjamini--Hochberg runs over the vision census and, separately, over the text census. The depth test uses the WordNet cut into thirty superclasses on ImageNet, the twenty coarse labels on CIFAR-100, a balanced frame as a control, and ten star seeds.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:form}

\subsection{The premise at the sample level}
\label{sec:f-sample}

\paragraph{The premise fails where it is read.} On per-image features the raw reading lies in the low band the literature reports. Calibrated against the matched null, it is not genuine in most cells. Where it is genuine, the excess removes at most two fifths of the null reading, most for DINOv2-G on CIFAR-100. The genuine cells belong mostly to the DINOv2 family, whose structure is the angular tree of Section~\ref{sec:content}. Table~\ref{tab:q3-sample} gives the reading per cell. % expR62_samplelevel_record.csv

\subsection{Clustered structure at the class level}
\label{sec:f-class}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_excess_final.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Most cells show clustered structure.} Excess of the reading of record over its matched null, one panel per dataset and one bar per backbone, filled when genuine and hatched when not, with the band of two null standard deviations around zero. % expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv
}
\label{fig:excess}
\end{figure}

\input{tab_census_final}

\paragraph{Most cells show clustered structure.} On class centroids the excess is negative in almost every cell. It is genuine in 49 of 72 cells. On ImageNet and CIFAR-100, the two sets with a real hierarchy, the count is 18 of 24. Figure~\ref{fig:excess} shows the census and Table~\ref{tab:census} gives it row by row. % expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv

\paragraph{The count survives resampling.} The census null accounts for replicate noise but not for image resampling or the estimator's seeds. Folding all three into one spread, or repeating the census on resampled centroid sets, leaves most of the count in place. The cells that move are on the transfer sets. Table~\ref{tab:q8-robust} gives the counts. % expR66_joint_sensitivity_summary.csv

\paragraph{No family owns the structure.} Every family contributes genuine cells. The smallest backbone is genuine on one dataset only, and the flat datasets sit near zero. A class-count control shows that the reading follows the hierarchy of the labels rather than their number. Two self-supervised ResNets read like the ViTs. Tables~\ref{tab:q1-census} and \ref{tab:q8-robust} give the details. % expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv, expR60_c_sweep_record.csv, expR45_convnet_rows.csv

\paragraph{A star of clusters already passes the census.} A star of clusters has structure beyond second moments, and a synthetic star with no hierarchy scores an excess as deep as any real cell. A negative excess therefore says clustered, not deep. Table~\ref{tab:q10-calibration} gives the star's excess. % expR42_star_calibration.csv

\paragraph{Neural collapse is the flat limit, not what the census sees.} Neural collapse predicts that supervised class means converge to a simplex frame \citep{papyan2020prevalence}, a star with no hierarchy. The arrangement we certify is not that frame: class means cluster around superclass hubs, and the same structure appears without labels and in language models. Scale deepens the excess for DINOv2 only, on CIFAR-10 and weakly on CIFAR-100. Training moves the raw reading within an architecture, up after fine-tuning without class hierarchy and down across depth. Table~\ref{tab:q8-robust} gives the training rows. % expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv, analysis4_finetuning.csv, e1_delta_by_layer.csv

\subsection{Depth above the labelled clusters}
\label{sec:f-depth}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_depth_final.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Depth above the superclasses is certified in {{N_IN}} of 12 ImageNet backbones, and the test never fires on randomized hubs.} (a) Depth $z$ per backbone under the matched star, filled and numbered when certified, hatched otherwise, with the certification line at $z=-2$. (b) Detection rate against implant strength on the real clouds with the real within-cluster spread (solid) and with it shrunk into the validated range (dashed). % expR56_depth_variants.csv, expR64b_wn30.csv
}
\label{fig:depth}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Four backbones are certified on ImageNet.} The real cloud is more hierarchical than its matched star in {{N_IN}} of 12 backbones, ViT-S, ViT-B, ViT-L and DINOv2-L, and never less. Under the spectrum-matched-hub star the certified set is the same four. The other eight are not detected, which is not the same as not hierarchical. Figure~\ref{fig:depth}a shows the $z$ per backbone and Table~\ref{tab:q4-depth} gives both stars. % expR56_depth_variants.csv, expR69_depth_haarhubs_summary.csv

\paragraph{The test never fires on randomized hubs.} With the hub arrangement of every real cloud randomized, the test fires in {{FA_HAAR}} runs, and the four certified backbones with random hubs never fire either. Their verdict therefore comes from the real hub arrangement. Table~\ref{tab:q5-power} gives the runs. % expR69_depth_haarhubs_summary.csv, expR64b_wn30.csv

\paragraph{The test is weak at ImageNet's noise level.} A clean implanted two-level tree is detected in almost no run, because the within-cluster spread of the real clouds exceeds their between-hub spread, outside the range the synthetic sweep covered. Once the offsets are shrunk into that range every backbone is detected, but two contrastive backbones then fire at zero strength, so the shrunk variant only measures power. Figure~\ref{fig:depth}b shows the detection rates and Table~\ref{tab:q5-power} gives the power. % expR64b_wn30.csv, expR64b_wn30_summary.csv

\paragraph{The certified set depends on the frame.} CIFAR-100 has a hundred centroids in twenty clusters, outside the validated regime, so its readings carry no certification. An earlier isotropic star produced false alarms and is withdrawn. Table~\ref{tab:q4-depth} gives the frames. % expR56_depth_variants.csv, expR64b_wn30bal_summary.csv

\paragraph{Leaf labels can produce the depth but do not guarantee it.} The augreg ViT-B trained on ImageNet-1k leaf labels is certified and aligned with WordNet like the ImageNet-21k ViTs. DeiT-B, trained on the same labels with another recipe, is neither certified nor aligned, yet it recovers the CIFAR-100 superclasses. A hierarchical label set is therefore not the explanation of the certified ViTs' depth, and alignment with the taxonomy is recipe-dependent. Table~\ref{tab:q4-depth} gives the two models. % expR70_inet1k_supervised.csv

\paragraph{Imposing the geometry does not create detected depth.} MERU embeds images on the Lorentz hyperboloid with an entailment objective, and its Euclidean twin shares backbone, data and recipe \citep{desai2023meru}. The two read the same excess, and neither is detected as more hierarchical than a matched star. MERU's embeddings never leave the near-flat regime, where Lorentz and Euclidean distances almost coincide, so the control tests the objective rather than a curved geometry. Table~\ref{tab:q4-depth} gives the readings and the radii. % expR63_meru_record.csv, expR71_meru_radii.csv

\subsection{Whose tree}
\label{sec:content}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{figures/fig_treemap_final.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{The island is a chaining artifact; a moderate gap remains, and the self-supervised tree lives in angles.} (a) Pairwise ARI between dendrogram cuts under the naive configuration, where the DINOv2 block agrees with the rest at {{NAIVE_BIG}}; (b) under the selected configuration, where it agrees at {{ARI_BIG}}; (c) sibling-triplet agreement under cosine and Euclidean distance: DINOv2 loses most of its structure under Euclidean distance, the other families do not. % exp23_treemap_controls.npz, exp10_local_vs_global.csv
}
\label{fig:treemap}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{The naive map manufactures an island.} Under Euclidean distances with average linkage the DINOv2 family agrees with the supervised and contrastive block far less than the block agrees with itself. The cause is chaining: DINOv2's Euclidean dendrograms put almost every class in one cluster, so the index is forced to zero. DINO-B does not fall in the island. Figure~\ref{fig:treemap}a shows the naive map. % exp23_treemap_controls.npz, exp23_config_diagnostics.json

\paragraph{Controlling the cut leaves a moderate gap.} The criterion of Section~\ref{sec:f-treemap} selects cosine distances with average linkage on ImageNet. Under it the island shrinks to a moderate gap, and about a third of the within-block agreement is missing under most admissible configurations. In cophenetic correlation the gap remains, and in triplet agreement, which needs no cut, it closes under cosine-average only. Figure~\ref{fig:treemap}b shows the selected map and Table~\ref{tab:q6-treemap} gives the six configurations. % exp23_treemap_controls.npz, expR58_treemap_cutfree_summary.csv

\paragraph{The self-supervised tree is angular.} DINOv2's semantics concentrate in the angular component of its representation: for DINOv2-L and DINOv2-G, sibling triplets are resolved far better under cosine than under Euclidean distance. This angular tree is why the Euclidean dendrograms chain. The census repeated on cosine geometry agrees with the Euclidean census in almost every cell, and DINOv2 on ImageNet is genuine under both. Figure~\ref{fig:treemap}c shows the triplets and Table~\ref{tab:q1-census} gives the cosine census. % exp10_local_vs_global.csv, expR57_census_cosine_haar_p999_200.csv

\paragraph{Alignment with WordNet follows supervision and recipe.} The correlation between inter-centroid and WordNet distances is highest for the contrastive VLMs, then the supervised ViTs, and lowest for DINOv2, with shuffle and Gaussian controls at zero. Supervision deepens the resemblance on average, and DeiT-B shows that the effect is recipe-dependent. The most aligned families carry the most fragile excess and DINOv2 the most robust one. Table~\ref{tab:q7-wordnet} gives the correlations. % exp3_alignment.csv, expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv

\paragraph{The recovery is not WordNet circularity.} On DBpedia Classes, an ontology independent of WordNet, the three text embedders read a genuine excess and recover the true partition under every configuration. On HierarCaps, a four-level caption hierarchy with no class set, the encoders resolve sibling triplets far above chance and order the levels by projection radius. Table~\ref{tab:q7-wordnet} gives both. % expR61_dbpedia_record.csv, exp27_dbpedia_treemap.json, exp16_hierarcaps.csv

\paragraph{Text depends on recipe, scale and probe.} On the ImageNet class names, {{TEXT_GEN}} of 15 text models are genuine under the record. GPT-2 S is genuine at the margin, GPT-2 M is not, and GPT-2 L and XL are genuine under every reading, as are Pythia at every scale and OLMo-1B. The sentence embedders are absent on the class names and present on DBpedia, so the probe decides their verdict. Prompt template and extraction move the fragile values but not the scale ordering. Table~\ref{tab:q2-text} gives the census. % expR53_text_haar_p999_200.csv, expR53_text_haar_sup_200.csv, expR57_text_cosine_haar_p999_200.csv

\subsection{What is shared is local}
\label{sec:f-local}

\paragraph{Models share neighborhoods, not metrics.} Permutation-calibrated mutual-kNN agreement and centroid CKA survive across the sixty-six model pairs, every pair significant. The Poincar\'{e} readout improves neither, so models share who is near whom and differ in the metric, the reading of \citet{groger2026aristotelian} on geometry. Table~\ref{tab:q13-local} gives the values. % exp21b_local_global_K200.csv

\section{Implications for Hyperbolic Representation Learning}
\label{sec:practice}

\paragraph{The raw reading cannot select a curvature.} \citet{Khrulkov_2020_CVPR} set the curvature of the Poincar\'{e} ball from the relative hyperbolicity,
\begin{equation}
c=\big(0.144/\delta_{\text{rel}}\big)^{2},\qquad \delta_{\text{rel}}=2\delta/\mathrm{diam}.
\label{eq:curvature}
\end{equation}
On the Gaussian band the rule assigns structureless clouds a curvature from {{C_LO}} to {{C_HI}}, tracking the width of the backbone rather than its hierarchy. Table~\ref{tab:q10-calibration} gives the band. % exp1_delta_controls.csv

\paragraph{The calibrated reading predicts the zero-cost gain.} Within each hierarchical dataset the record excess predicts the nearest-centroid gain of the Poincar\'{e} readout on all four datasets and the few-shot gain on three. The depth verdict predicts no gain. The prediction describes the families it is scored on rather than validating a policy. Table~\ref{tab:q9-corollary} gives the correlations. % expR68_corollary_excess.csv

\paragraph{A change of readout collects what is there.} Cosine collects the self-supervised structure, and the Poincar\'{e} readout adds ${{GAIN_LO}}$ to ${{GAIN_HI}}$ pp over cosine for the contrastive VLMs on prototype tasks and nothing consistent elsewhere. Nothing helps on flat labels, and sample-level tasks never benefit. Before imposing curvature, read the excess and its rank, the depth test, and the cosine reading. Table~\ref{tab:q9-corollary} gives the gains. % exp2b_normalized_stack.csv, exp2_metric_controls.csv, exp24_val_metric_selection.csv

\section{Conclusion and Limitations}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Conclusion.} Read correctly, foundation models organize classes into clustered structure that is occasionally hierarchical and moderately shared; they do not converge to one common tree, and their raw tree-likeness is not evidence for hyperbolic geometry. The premise of latent hyperbolicity is confounded, and its sample-level reading sits within null noise. Curvature should be measured rather than assumed, and a change of readout collects what is there.

\paragraph{Limitations.} (i)~The excess certifies structure beyond second moments, star-like clustering included, so depth needs the separate test. (ii)~The depth test is conservative and weak at ImageNet's noise level, certifies hierarchy above its frame only, leaves hierarchy within the clusters untested, and its one trained positive control is inconclusive. (iii)~A causal LM's class representation depends on the extraction protocol as much as on the model, and its values are template-conditioned. (iv)~The census is class-centroid geometry on six datasets, and the sample-level reading covers two datasets and one image budget. (v)~The objective--geometry link is correlational, resting chiefly on DINOv2's scale range and on two leaf-label ViTs. (vi)~Downstream scope is prototype-based metric tasks with modest gains.

